# Universe content · geology: earthquakes and faults, plate edges, erosion, soil and the rock cycle
# researched: Wikipedia "Earthquake", "Moment magnitude scale", "Gutenberg–Richter law", "Fault (geology)",
#   "Plate tectonics", "Rock cycle", "Erosion", "Denudation", "Soil formation";
#   LibreTexts "How Fast Are the Continents Worn Down" (Southard); Litvin 1994 denudation rates;
#   Wilkinson 2009 (AGU) continental erosion; Willenbring et al. 2014 (Earth Surface Dynamics); Wilner et al. 2024 glacial erosion
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md · docs/TODO.md R6b

# ── Plate edges ──

@edge divergent
name: Spreading edge
real: yes
says: two plates pull apart; magma rises to fill the gap and makes new crust (mid-ocean ridges, rift valleys)
quakes: many small and shallow
volcanoes: gentle, runny lava
example: Mid-Atlantic Ridge, East African Rift

@edge convergent
name: Crashing edge
real: yes
says: two plates push together; one dives under the other into a trench (subduction), or both crumple into mountains
quakes: the biggest on the planet, shallow to very deep
volcanoes: explosive, ash-rich, in chains above the diving plate
example: the Andes, Japan, the Himalayas (continent against continent)

@edge transform
name: Sliding edge
real: yes
says: two plates grind past each other sideways; crust is neither made nor destroyed
quakes: strong and shallow
volcanoes: few or none
example: San Andreas Fault

@edge hotspot
name: Hot spot
real: yes
says: a plume of hot rock rising from deep in the mantle, away from plate edges; a plate moving over it leaves a chain of volcanoes
example: Hawaii, Iceland, Yellowstone

# ── Faults ──

@fault normal
name: Normal fault
real: yes
says: the crust stretches and one side drops down
found-at: spreading edges

@fault reverse
name: Reverse (thrust) fault
real: yes
says: the crust is squeezed and one side is pushed up over the other
found-at: crashing edges

@fault strike-slip
name: Strike-slip fault
real: yes
says: the two sides slide past each other sideways
found-at: sliding edges

# ── Earthquakes ──

# moment magnitude: each step up releases about 32 times more energy; quake counts fall about tenfold per step (Gutenberg–Richter, b about 1)

@quake great
name: Great earthquake
real: yes
magnitude: 8..9.5
per-year-earth: 1
says: devastates a whole region; undersea ones send tsunamis across oceans
largest-recorded: magnitude 9.5, Chile 1960

@quake major
name: Major earthquake
real: yes
magnitude: 7..7.9
per-year-earth: 15
says: serious damage over a large area

@quake strong
name: Strong earthquake
real: yes
magnitude: 6..6.9
per-year-earth: 134
says: damage in populated areas

@quake moderate
name: Moderate earthquake
real: yes
magnitude: 5..5.9
per-year-earth: 1300
says: felt widely, damage to weak buildings

@quake minor
name: Minor earthquake
real: yes
magnitude: 3..4.9
per-year-earth: 130000
says: felt, rarely harmful

@hazard tsunami
name: Tsunami
real: yes
says: a sea wave from an undersea quake, landslide or impact; it crosses oceans at jet speed and rises tall at the coast

@hazard landslide
name: Landslide
real: yes
says: a slope gives way after rain, quakes or erosion

# ── Wearing down ──

@erosion rain-and-rivers
name: Rain and rivers
real: yes
rate-mm-per-year: 0.02..0.08
says: the main way land wears down; rivers carry the pieces to the sea
mountains: about ten times faster in high, steep, wet ranges, up to a few mm a year

@erosion ice
name: Glaciers
real: yes
rate-mm-per-year: 0.5
says: moving ice grinds valleys into U shapes, about ten times faster than rivers

@erosion wind
name: Wind
real: yes
says: wind blasts sand and lifts dust in dry lands; dunes and dust storms

@erosion waves
name: Waves
real: yes
says: the sea eats coasts back into cliffs and beaches

@erosion farming
name: Farmed land
real: yes
rate-mm-per-year: 0.6
says: ploughed fields wear away many times faster than wild land

@balance uplift
name: Uplift and wearing down
real: yes
says: active mountain ranges rise about 1 mm a year and wear down at about the same rate; when the push stops, a range wears flat in tens of millions of years

# ── Soil ──

@soil forming
name: Soil forming
real: yes
says: rock breaks down by heat, cold, water and chemistry; roots, fungi, worms and microbes mix it with dead life into soil
rate: a few centimetres per thousand years; faster where warm, wet and alive

@soil layers
name: Soil layers
real: yes
says: dead leaves on top, dark living topsoil, paler subsoil, then broken rock above the bedrock

# ── The rock cycle ──

@rock igneous
name: Igneous rock
real: yes
says: made when magma or lava cools (granite, basalt)

@rock sedimentary
name: Sedimentary rock
real: yes
says: made when sand, mud, shells and dead life are buried and pressed into stone (sandstone, limestone, chalk, coal)

@rock metamorphic
name: Metamorphic rock
real: yes
says: rock changed by deep heat and pressure without melting (marble, slate)

@rule rock-cycle
real: rule
says: rock is made, worn down, buried and pressed into new rock, cooked into changed rock, melted to magma and cooled again; plate edges carry it down and push it back up

@rule quake-edges
real: rule
says: earthquakes happen along plate edges and faults; counts fall tenfold for each step of magnitude; crashing edges make the biggest; undersea quakes can make tsunamis

@rule erosion-climate
real: rule
says: wetter, icier, steeper and higher ground wears down faster; plants hold soil, bare and farmed ground loses it

@rule soil-life
real: rule
says: soil comes from worn rock and dead life; life speeds it up; without soil, land plants cannot spread
